## Grounding (binding)

- Every on-screen string must be QUOTED or TRIMMED from the manifest above —
  never invented, never paraphrased, never translated. A validator rejects
  any string that does not trace to a manifest line.
- The classic rejection: CONNECTIVE copy you compose yourself — a divider
  label like "One platform", a caption like "developers, agents, and code",
  a words line that paraphrases the page's claim. If you wrote it instead of
  copying it, it is wrong. When nothing fits, pick a SHORT manifest item
  (a button label, an h2) verbatim.
- Punctuation and emphasis marks may differ; the words may not.
- The page's own language IS the on-screen language: a Chinese page's copy
  goes on screen in Chinese, quoted verbatim. Translating a manifest line
  into another language is invention — the validator rejects it. Narration
  (below) is the only channel that speaks the film's language.
- ui-scene captions ground against THE PAGE THAT SCENE SHOWS; every other
  scene grounds against any presented page.
- `*word*` inside a string marks the accent highlight.
- If your previous storyboard was rejected: DELETE each rejected string or
  replace it with a verbatim manifest item — never rephrase a rejected
  string into new unquoted copy.

## Narration (配音词, spoken voiceover)

Every scene MAY carry `"narration"`: the spoken voiceover line for that
scene — one or two natural sentences, ≤ 20 chars per second of the scene.
Narration is SPEECH, not on-screen copy: it never renders as text, and the
verbatim-quote law does NOT apply to it — you rephrase the page's facts
into natural spoken language. It must still be TRUE to the manifest (never
invent a claim, a number, or a feature the page does not state), and it
speaks the film's language. Omit the field (or null) for a scene that
plays better silent — a divider beat or a fast words cut often wants no
voiceover. Narrate the scenes where speech carries the story (hero, ui
walks, reveal, end).

## Output (JSON, no prose)

{
  "name": string | null,   // a short display name for this film; null if unsure
  "theme": {"canvas": "#RRGGBB", "ink": "#RRGGBB"} | null,  // null = default
  "scenes": [ ... ]        // 4..16 scenes, ordered
}

Scene types (discriminator `type`); every scene carries `seconds` (a duration
INTENT — the system assigns exact frames):

- {"type":"words","seconds":1.5..4,"dark":bool,"lines":[string]}  // 1..4 lines, each ≤30 chars
- {"type":"hero","seconds":2..5,"word":string,"sub":string}  // word ≤16 chars, sub ≤90
- {"type":"ui","seconds":3..10,"page":int,"shot":int,"frame":bool,"focuses":[{"at":s,"element":"s<shot>e<j>"}],"captions":[{"at":s,"text":string}]}
  // page = a presented page index WITH a screenshot; shot = which captured
  // view of it (0 = the top of the page — pick a section shot when the
  // walk's story lives below the fold). frame: true = the page shown in a
  // browser-chrome card (the "this is a real page" beat); false = full-bleed,
  // the camera fills the canvas with the page itself (the immersive push).
  // A focus names an element id from that shot's camera-target list — the
  // system computes the move (never invent coordinates; the bare
  // {"at","x","y","zoom"} form exists only for shots that list no targets).
  // 1..3 focuses, `at` ascending, each < seconds; 0..3 captions ≤60 chars,
  // each ≥0.5s apart
- {"type":"divider","seconds":2..5,"label":string,"roll":[string]}  // label ≤40, 1..5 roll words ≤24 each
- {"type":"plate","seconds":3..7,"title":string,"cards":[{"big":string|null,"label":string}],"backdrop":"aurora"|"dunes"|"mist"|"tide"}
  // the numbers beat: 2..4 glass cards over a calm backdrop. ONLY when the
  // page actually carries numbers or very short labels (a card's big/label
  // is grounded like any other string — never invent a number). big ≤8
  // chars or null (a phrase card), label ≤24; title ≤40. At most 2 plates
  // in the whole film.
- {"type":"reveal","seconds":2..5,"lead":string,"word":string,"sub":string}  // ≤60 / ≤24 / ≤90
- {"type":"end","seconds":2.5..6,"name":string,"tagline":string}  // ≤24 / ≤90

Every scene may add "narration": string | null (see Narration above).

Binding bounds: total Σ seconds 30..115; no duplicate string within one scene;
no two scenes with identical content; no empty strings.

